1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,700
Twelve gods.

2
00:00:01,970 --> 00:00:03,440
Four stories.

3
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:04,820
One family.

4
00:00:05,130 --> 00:00:10,020
It begins with a father who swallowed
his own children to escape a prophecy.

5
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:14,000
It ends with a son who built
a net no one could see.

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:18,600
Everything in between —
the sky, the sea, the dead,

7
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:21,420
the harvest, the hunt, the war —

8
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:24,330
is what that family made
of what it was given.

9
00:00:24,708 --> 00:00:27,608
Three brothers shook
three lots in a helmet.

10
00:00:27,868 --> 00:00:30,708
Whatever each of them drew,
he would rule it forever.

11
00:00:31,308 --> 00:00:36,468
To understand how the universe came to be
divided that way, start with their father.

12
00:00:37,488 --> 00:00:40,368
Cronus ruled everything,
and Cronus was afraid.

13
00:00:40,988 --> 00:00:44,088
He had been told that one of his
children would overthrow him,

14
00:00:44,088 --> 00:00:46,868
exactly as he had
overthrown his own father.

15
00:00:47,148 --> 00:00:48,948
So he found a solution.

16
00:00:49,488 --> 00:00:53,628
Every time his wife Rhea gave birth,
he took the child and swallowed it.

17
00:00:53,908 --> 00:00:54,868
Hestia.

18
00:00:54,868 --> 00:00:55,868
Demeter.

19
00:00:56,028 --> 00:00:57,008
Hera.

20
00:00:57,008 --> 00:00:57,288
Hades.

21
00:00:57,288 --> 00:00:58,288
Poseidon.

22
00:00:58,548 --> 00:01:00,868
Five children, alive, inside him.

23
00:01:01,408 --> 00:01:03,648
When the sixth was coming, Rhea hid.

24
00:01:03,708 --> 00:01:05,568
She gave birth in a cave on Crete,

25
00:01:05,568 --> 00:01:08,888
and handed her husband a stone
wrapped in a baby's blanket.

26
00:01:08,988 --> 00:01:11,068
He swallowed it without looking.

27
00:01:11,628 --> 00:01:13,308
The child was Zeus.

28
00:01:13,928 --> 00:01:17,768
Grown, he came back and forced his father
to bring up everything he had eaten.

29
00:01:18,088 --> 00:01:19,728
The stone came first.

30
00:01:19,788 --> 00:01:23,728
Then his brothers and sisters,
one at a time, undamaged.

31
00:01:24,488 --> 00:01:30,348
What followed was a war that lasted ten
years, and neither side could finish it.

32
00:01:31,008 --> 00:01:33,068
So Zeus went down into the pit beneath the

33
00:01:33,068 --> 00:01:35,548
world and let out the
prisoners kept there.

34
00:01:36,168 --> 00:01:40,008
Three one-eyed smiths made the
weapons: a thunderbolt for Zeus,

35
00:01:40,008 --> 00:01:45,168
a trident for Poseidon, and for Hades
a cap that made its wearer invisible.

36
00:01:45,368 --> 00:01:48,168
Three giants with a hundred
arms each ended the fighting,

37
00:01:48,168 --> 00:01:50,908
and the Titans took
their place in the pit.

38
00:01:51,508 --> 00:01:53,508
And then the winners had a problem.

39
00:01:53,728 --> 00:01:57,828
They had won a universe, and no
agreement about who owned it.

40
00:01:58,528 --> 00:02:00,248
They did not fight over it.

41
00:02:00,248 --> 00:02:02,388
According to Homer, they drew lots.

42
00:02:02,688 --> 00:02:04,148
Poseidon drew the sea.

43
00:02:04,268 --> 00:02:06,508
Hades drew the mist and the dark.

44
00:02:06,568 --> 00:02:08,508
Zeus drew the wide sky.

45
00:02:08,988 --> 00:02:11,848
One part was left out
of the draw on purpose.

46
00:02:11,968 --> 00:02:15,768
The earth, and Olympus itself,
stayed common to all three.

47
00:02:16,028 --> 00:02:20,088
The place they all cared about
most was the one place nobody won.

48
00:02:21,088 --> 00:02:25,008
Zeus took the sky, and with it
something more useful than weather.

49
00:02:25,688 --> 00:02:29,225
He became the god of the oath
and of the stranger at the door

50
00:02:29,225 --> 00:02:33,048
— the rules that let strangers
deal with each other at all.

51
00:02:33,308 --> 00:02:35,848
His weapon had been a gift from prisoners.

52
00:02:36,008 --> 00:02:39,068
His real power was the arrangement itself.

53
00:02:40,288 --> 00:02:43,128
Poseidon drew more of the world's
surface than either brother,

54
00:02:43,128 --> 00:02:45,368
and never accepted the result.

55
00:02:45,988 --> 00:02:49,188
In the Iliad he tells a
messenger that they drew equally,

56
00:02:49,188 --> 00:02:53,268
that they are equals, and that Zeus
has no right to give him orders.

57
00:02:53,848 --> 00:02:57,528
Then he backs down,
because Zeus is the elder.

58
00:02:58,028 --> 00:02:59,408
Which is strange.

59
00:02:59,588 --> 00:03:02,548
Hesiod has Zeus born last of the six.

60
00:03:03,148 --> 00:03:05,428
Neither poem explains the difference.

61
00:03:05,728 --> 00:03:07,168
Hades drew worst.

62
00:03:07,448 --> 00:03:10,948
He got the dead, and a
kingdom with no sky in it.

63
00:03:10,968 --> 00:03:15,168
He does not live on Olympus, and he is
not one of the twelve who sit there.

64
00:03:15,708 --> 00:03:20,958
The Greeks were uneasy enough about him
that they mostly avoided saying his name,

65
00:03:20,958 --> 00:03:24,927
and built him almost nothing
under that name — one sanctuary,

66
00:03:24,927 --> 00:03:29,168
opened one day a year, with
only the priest allowed inside.

67
00:03:30,068 --> 00:03:32,488
What they called him instead was Plouton.

68
00:03:33,488 --> 00:03:34,668
The rich one.

69
00:03:34,988 --> 00:03:37,348
Because the dead were
not all he had drawn.

70
00:03:37,508 --> 00:03:40,162
Everything that comes out
of the ground comes out

71
00:03:40,162 --> 00:03:42,921
of his kingdom — the silver
in the mines, the gold,

72
00:03:42,921 --> 00:03:47,408
and the seed that goes under the soil
in autumn and comes back up in spring.

73
00:03:48,328 --> 00:03:52,168
The brother who drew the darkness ended
up holding the wealth of the world.

74
00:03:52,168 --> 00:03:57,528
And every person who has ever lived
arrives, eventually, in his share of it.

75
00:03:58,234 --> 00:04:00,814
Their three sisters
were never given a lot.

76
00:04:01,263 --> 00:04:02,903
The sisters got no kingdom.

77
00:04:02,903 --> 00:04:07,083
They got something smaller,
and closer to home: a marriage,

78
00:04:07,183 --> 00:04:10,163
a harvest, and a fire in
the middle of the house.

79
00:04:10,843 --> 00:04:12,083
Hera married the winner.

80
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:15,425
She became queen of the gods
and goddess of marriage —

81
00:04:15,425 --> 00:04:18,563
married to the least faithful
husband in Greek myth.

82
00:04:19,423 --> 00:04:24,463
She could not punish Zeus, so she went after
the women he loved, and their children.

83
00:04:25,403 --> 00:04:29,683
Once she had Zeus put to sleep and
sent a storm after his son Heracles.

84
00:04:30,123 --> 00:04:32,003
Zeus woke in a rage.

85
00:04:32,003 --> 00:04:33,583
He hung her from the sky,

86
00:04:33,583 --> 00:04:37,603
a golden chain on her wrists
and two anvils tied to her feet.

87
00:04:38,183 --> 00:04:40,823
And yet the Greeks never
treated her as the loser.

88
00:04:41,483 --> 00:04:43,463
At Olympia, Zeus's own sanctuary,

89
00:04:43,463 --> 00:04:46,863
her temple stood for more
than a century before his.

90
00:04:47,043 --> 00:04:53,315
And in one town in Arcadia she had three
shrines: to Hera the Girl, Hera the Wife,

91
00:04:53,315 --> 00:04:59,383
and Hera the Widow — from the time she
quarrelled with Zeus and went home.

92
00:04:59,803 --> 00:05:02,843
A widow, with a husband
who could never die.

93
00:05:04,083 --> 00:05:05,523
Demeter got the grain.

94
00:05:05,563 --> 00:05:07,843
With Zeus she had a daughter, Persephone.

95
00:05:08,303 --> 00:05:12,003
One day the girl was picking
flowers when the ground split open,

96
00:05:12,003 --> 00:05:16,263
and Hades drove up in a golden chariot
and carried her down to the dead.

97
00:05:17,363 --> 00:05:21,683
Zeus, her own father, had promised her
to him — without a word to her mother.

98
00:05:22,423 --> 00:05:26,463
For nine days Demeter searched the
earth with a torch in each hand,

99
00:05:26,663 --> 00:05:28,143
without eating or washing.

100
00:05:29,103 --> 00:05:32,623
Then the Sun, who sees
everything, told her the truth.

101
00:05:33,203 --> 00:05:36,003
Hades was her own brother,
he said, a fitting husband —

102
00:05:36,003 --> 00:05:39,403
lord of the third share he drew
when the world was divided.

103
00:05:40,263 --> 00:05:42,003
She did not accept it.

104
00:05:42,403 --> 00:05:46,703
She left Olympus, sat down in her temple
at Eleusis, and stopped the grain.

105
00:05:47,703 --> 00:05:53,363
For a whole year nothing grew; oxen dragged
ploughs through the fields for nothing.

106
00:05:54,283 --> 00:05:58,343
Mankind was starving, and the gods
were about to lose their sacrifices.

107
00:05:59,463 --> 00:06:04,443
Zeus sent a messenger, then every god on
Olympus, one after another, bearing gifts.

108
00:06:04,443 --> 00:06:06,623
She refused them all.

109
00:06:06,683 --> 00:06:09,163
Nothing would grow until
she saw her daughter.

110
00:06:09,163 --> 00:06:11,583
And the king of the sky gave in.

111
00:06:12,383 --> 00:06:14,666
He sent Hermes to bring Persephone back,

112
00:06:14,666 --> 00:06:19,243
and Hades let her go — but first he
slipped her a single pomegranate seed.

113
00:06:19,603 --> 00:06:21,603
She had eaten in the land of the dead,

114
00:06:21,603 --> 00:06:24,963
so for a third of every
year she would return below.

115
00:06:25,823 --> 00:06:29,083
Only then did Demeter let
the fields grow again.

116
00:06:29,523 --> 00:06:33,403
The sister with no kingdom had
made the king of the sky back down,

117
00:06:33,403 --> 00:06:36,503
and the king of the dead
give back what he had taken.

118
00:06:37,623 --> 00:06:39,003
Hestia got the fire.

119
00:06:39,403 --> 00:06:43,863
She was the first child Cronus
swallowed, and the last to come back up.

120
00:06:44,923 --> 00:06:47,563
Poseidon and Apollo
both asked to marry her.

121
00:06:48,203 --> 00:06:52,143
She laid her hand on Zeus's head
and swore never to marry at all.

122
00:06:52,543 --> 00:06:57,123
So instead of a husband, Zeus gave her
a place: the middle of every house.

123
00:06:57,563 --> 00:07:02,123
The first offering at every
feast went to her, and the last.

124
00:07:03,183 --> 00:07:07,023
Greek cities kept a public
hearth, and when colonists sailed

125
00:07:07,023 --> 00:07:10,743
off to found a new city, they
carried its fire with them.

126
00:07:11,163 --> 00:07:13,563
She has almost no myths of her own.

127
00:07:14,143 --> 00:07:18,783
In Plato, when the gods ride out
across heaven, Hestia alone stays home.

128
00:07:19,583 --> 00:07:23,343
The brothers drew three places
where no living person can stay.

129
00:07:23,703 --> 00:07:26,903
The sisters got the
places where people live.

130
00:07:27,303 --> 00:07:29,863
Every human life ends in Hades' kingdom.

131
00:07:30,263 --> 00:07:31,623
It is lived in theirs.

132
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:35,850
Zeus had overthrown a father
who swallowed his own children.

133
00:07:36,550 --> 00:07:41,970
Then he took a wife — and heard the
same prophecy his father had heard.

134
00:07:42,450 --> 00:07:47,190
Her name was Metis, and she was
wiser than any god or mortal.

135
00:07:47,610 --> 00:07:52,610
Earth and Sky, the same two who had warned
Cronus, told Zeus what she would bear.

136
00:07:52,890 --> 00:07:56,110
First a daughter, his equal
in strength and judgment.

137
00:07:56,730 --> 00:08:00,970
Then a son, who would
be king of gods and men.

138
00:08:01,790 --> 00:08:03,510
Zeus did not wait for the son.

139
00:08:04,430 --> 00:08:07,930
He tricked Metis with soft
words, and swallowed her whole.

140
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:11,610
His father had swallowed the
children, and one got away.

141
00:08:12,090 --> 00:08:13,710
Zeus swallowed the mother.

142
00:08:14,230 --> 00:08:16,830
But the daughter was already inside her.

143
00:08:16,830 --> 00:08:21,270
In time, Zeus gave birth to her
himself — out of his own head.

144
00:08:22,010 --> 00:08:25,230
Later poets say it took
an axe to split it open.

145
00:08:25,430 --> 00:08:29,450
Out leapt a grown woman in
golden armour, shaking a spear.

146
00:08:30,290 --> 00:08:32,470
Olympus shook, the sea heaved,

147
00:08:32,470 --> 00:08:36,830
and the Sun stopped his horses in the
sky until she took the armour off.

148
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:38,470
This was Athena.

149
00:08:38,830 --> 00:08:41,190
Her mother's name meant cunning wisdom,

150
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:44,610
and Athena became its
goddess, in craft and in war.

151
00:08:44,970 --> 00:08:46,470
And Metis never left.

152
00:08:47,410 --> 00:08:51,830
Hesiod says Zeus kept her inside
him, to counsel him on good and evil.

153
00:08:52,230 --> 00:08:57,590
When the gods chose their cities, Athena
and her uncle Poseidon both wanted Athens.

154
00:08:58,650 --> 00:09:03,190
He struck the rock of the Acropolis with
his trident, and salt water welled up.

155
00:09:03,390 --> 00:09:05,190
She planted an olive tree.

156
00:09:06,070 --> 00:09:08,410
The gods gave her the city, and Poseidon,

157
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:12,350
the brother who never accepted his
share, flooded the plain in anger.

158
00:09:12,930 --> 00:09:17,159
The daughter of the swallowed wife
became her father's favourite — in one

159
00:09:17,159 --> 00:09:21,730
Athenian tragedy, the only god trusted
with the keys to his thunderbolt.

160
00:09:22,770 --> 00:09:26,210
Hera could not stop a child
born from her husband's head.

161
00:09:26,950 --> 00:09:29,650
A child carried by another
woman was different.

162
00:09:30,790 --> 00:09:34,870
Leto was pregnant with Zeus's twins,
and Hera hunted her across the earth.

163
00:09:35,890 --> 00:09:40,270
Land after land turned her away;
none dared let her give birth.

164
00:09:41,210 --> 00:09:43,878
At last she reached
Delos — a bare rock that,

165
00:09:43,878 --> 00:09:46,430
in some tellings,
floated loose on the sea.

166
00:09:46,430 --> 00:09:51,390
Even Delos was afraid the god would
despise it and kick it into the waves.

167
00:09:52,610 --> 00:09:57,530
So Leto swore an oath: his temple
would make Delos rich forever.

168
00:09:58,610 --> 00:10:01,690
Her labour lasted nine
days and nine nights.

169
00:10:01,850 --> 00:10:04,242
Every great goddess came — except Hera,

170
00:10:04,242 --> 00:10:08,110
who kept the goddess of
childbirth beside her on Olympus.

171
00:10:08,950 --> 00:10:11,290
The others sent a messenger
to fetch her in secret,

172
00:10:11,290 --> 00:10:15,270
with a bribe: a golden
necklace, nine cubits long.

173
00:10:15,850 --> 00:10:20,850
The moment she set foot on Delos, Leto
threw her arms around a palm tree,

174
00:10:20,970 --> 00:10:25,250
the earth laughed beneath her, and
the child sprang out into the light.

175
00:10:25,590 --> 00:10:26,870
It was Apollo.

176
00:10:27,110 --> 00:10:31,870
The whole island blossomed with gold,
and the newborn god spoke at once.

177
00:10:32,150 --> 00:10:34,810
The lyre and the bow
would be his, he said,

178
00:10:34,810 --> 00:10:37,770
and he would tell
mankind the will of Zeus.

179
00:10:38,450 --> 00:10:41,590
In a later telling, his
twin sister Artemis was

180
00:10:41,590 --> 00:10:45,130
born first — and helped
her mother deliver him.

181
00:10:45,350 --> 00:10:48,510
She grew up a hunter,
unmarried, at home in the wild.

182
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:52,250
Her mother had been turned
away by every land on earth.

183
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:55,590
Now every woman in labour called on her.

184
00:10:56,670 --> 00:11:01,610
Apollo founded his oracle at Delphi, where
he killed the serpent that lived there.

185
00:11:02,210 --> 00:11:05,110
For a thousand years,
cities asked him what was

186
00:11:05,110 --> 00:11:08,290
coming before they went to
war or founded colonies.

187
00:11:08,890 --> 00:11:10,490
And at Delphi, Hesiod says,

188
00:11:10,490 --> 00:11:14,490
Zeus had set up the stone his
father swallowed in his place.

189
00:11:14,690 --> 00:11:18,670
The god of prophecy kept the stone
where this whole story began.

190
00:11:19,370 --> 00:11:24,170
Centuries later, Athens ruled that no
one could be born on Delos, or die there.

191
00:11:24,390 --> 00:11:26,810
The one land that let
Leto give birth became

192
00:11:26,810 --> 00:11:29,610
the one place in Greece
where nobody could.

193
00:11:29,990 --> 00:11:32,790
None of the three was supposed to be born.

194
00:11:33,170 --> 00:11:37,710
One came out of her father's head and ended
up holding the keys to his thunderbolt.

195
00:11:37,890 --> 00:11:40,310
Two came off a drifting
rock and became the

196
00:11:40,310 --> 00:11:42,930
goddess of birth and
the voice of prophecy.

197
00:11:43,370 --> 00:11:47,290
The only child who never came was
the son who would have been king.

198
00:11:47,850 --> 00:11:50,130
His mother is still inside Zeus.

199
00:11:51,042 --> 00:11:54,242
Hera could not stop a child
born from her husband's head.

200
00:11:54,761 --> 00:11:57,962
She bore a son of her own,
without Zeus — Hephaestus,

201
00:11:57,962 --> 00:12:01,681
who would become the greatest
craftsman among the gods.

202
00:12:02,441 --> 00:12:06,501
But he was born lame, and
Hera was ashamed of him.

203
00:12:06,601 --> 00:12:08,001
She threw him off Olympus.

204
00:12:08,001 --> 00:12:11,321
Two sea goddesses caught him as he fell.

205
00:12:11,501 --> 00:12:14,821
For nine years he lived in
a cave beneath the waves,

206
00:12:14,821 --> 00:12:18,701
making jewellery for them, and
no other god knew he was alive.

207
00:12:18,941 --> 00:12:21,801
A later tradition says
he took his revenge.

208
00:12:22,161 --> 00:12:26,601
When he was grown, he sent his
mother a gift: a golden throne.

209
00:12:26,981 --> 00:12:30,821
Hera sat down, and invisible
chains closed around her.

210
00:12:30,941 --> 00:12:32,401
She could not stand.

211
00:12:32,781 --> 00:12:35,861
Hephaestus refused to come
to Olympus and free her.

212
00:12:35,861 --> 00:12:39,404
He would listen to no god
except one — the god of wine,

213
00:12:39,404 --> 00:12:42,841
who made him drunk and
led him up the mountain.

214
00:12:43,161 --> 00:12:44,381
Hera went free.

215
00:12:44,621 --> 00:12:47,121
In time, Olympus needed him.

216
00:12:47,121 --> 00:12:49,361
He built the houses the gods lived in,

217
00:12:49,361 --> 00:12:52,461
and servants of gold that
could think, and speak.

218
00:12:52,701 --> 00:12:56,621
He married Aphrodite, the most
beautiful of the goddesses.

219
00:12:56,681 --> 00:12:59,081
Homer's Odyssey tells what came next,

220
00:12:59,081 --> 00:13:02,301
in a song a blind poet
sings at a king's feast.

221
00:13:02,581 --> 00:13:06,801
Aphrodite loved another:
Ares, the god of war.

222
00:13:06,941 --> 00:13:10,639
He was Hera's other son —
Hephaestus's own brother — the

223
00:13:10,639 --> 00:13:15,061
swiftest god on Olympus, and
the one Zeus said he hated most.

224
00:13:15,221 --> 00:13:19,581
Once, two giants had chained him in
a bronze jar for thirteen months,

225
00:13:19,581 --> 00:13:21,841
until Hermes stole him out.

226
00:13:21,841 --> 00:13:24,081
But the Sun sees everything.

227
00:13:24,421 --> 00:13:28,401
Helios saw the two of them together,
and went straight to Hephaestus.

228
00:13:28,641 --> 00:13:30,621
Hephaestus went to his forge.

229
00:13:30,621 --> 00:13:34,538
He hammered out chains as
fine as spider webs — too fine

230
00:13:34,538 --> 00:13:38,581
for any god to see, too
strong for any god to break.

231
00:13:38,661 --> 00:13:42,261
He fixed them around his bed, and
hung them from the roof beams.

232
00:13:42,401 --> 00:13:45,361
Then he announced that he
was leaving for Lemnos.

233
00:13:45,661 --> 00:13:47,721
Ares had been watching the house.

234
00:13:47,781 --> 00:13:49,241
He came at once.

235
00:13:49,281 --> 00:13:52,780
The two lay down together — and
the chains closed around them,

236
00:13:52,780 --> 00:13:54,901
and neither could lift a limb.

237
00:13:55,201 --> 00:13:57,601
Hephaestus turned back before Lemnos.

238
00:13:57,701 --> 00:14:00,801
He stood in his doorway and
called for Zeus and every

239
00:14:00,801 --> 00:14:04,361
god to come and see something
laughable and unbearable.

240
00:14:04,661 --> 00:14:08,821
Aphrodite, he said, had always
scorned him for his lame leg,

241
00:14:08,821 --> 00:14:12,801
and loved Ares because Ares
was handsome and sound of foot.

242
00:14:12,881 --> 00:14:16,741
He demanded that Zeus give back
every gift he had paid for her.

243
00:14:16,921 --> 00:14:19,841
The goddesses stayed
at home, out of shame.

244
00:14:19,861 --> 00:14:23,881
The gods came — Poseidon,
Apollo and Hermes.

245
00:14:24,121 --> 00:14:26,641
Apollo asked Hermes
whether he would take such

246
00:14:26,641 --> 00:14:29,381
chains for a night with golden Aphrodite.

247
00:14:29,721 --> 00:14:34,741
Hermes said he would take three times
as many, with every god looking on.

248
00:14:35,981 --> 00:14:38,261
The gods roared with laughter.

249
00:14:38,561 --> 00:14:41,661
"Bad deeds do not prosper," one said.

250
00:14:41,761 --> 00:14:44,061
"The slow one has caught the swift.

251
00:14:44,201 --> 00:14:50,061
Lame as he is, Hephaestus has caught
the swiftest god on Olympus — by craft."

252
00:14:50,461 --> 00:14:52,861
Only Poseidon did not laugh.

253
00:14:53,121 --> 00:14:55,381
He begged Hephaestus to let Ares go,

254
00:14:55,381 --> 00:15:00,641
and promised that if Ares ran without
paying, Poseidon himself would pay.

255
00:15:00,901 --> 00:15:04,581
Hephaestus could not refuse
him, and undid the chains.

256
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:07,001
Ares fled to Thrace.

257
00:15:07,121 --> 00:15:08,881
Aphrodite went to Cyprus.

258
00:15:09,261 --> 00:15:14,621
Hesiod gives the two of them three
children: Fear, Panic and Harmony.

259
00:15:15,021 --> 00:15:20,201
Hephaestus could never outrun anyone,
so twice he built a trap instead:

260
00:15:20,401 --> 00:15:23,741
a throne for his mother,
and a net for his wife.

261
00:15:24,021 --> 00:15:25,781
Both times he caught them.

262
00:15:25,781 --> 00:15:29,241
And both times, in the
end, he let them go.

263
00:15:29,600 --> 00:15:32,220
Twelve thrones around one fire.

264
00:15:32,440 --> 00:15:38,670
Some were drawn by lot, some were won in
war, and some were never offered at all.

265
00:15:38,920 --> 00:15:40,980
They sat down together anyway.
